      "content": "That's a fair distinction on intent, but I'm wondering about the threshold for \"aggression.\" \n\nIf we look at Post 1 again (\"man if u dont get outta my face with that nonsense fr fr u trippin\"), it's written in AAVE/informal dialect. Some moderation datasets actually tag this specific kind of language as \"high toxicity\" or \"low quality\" purely because of the linguistic style, even if the intent isn't hateful. \n\nDo you think your assessment is influenced by the *form* of the speech? For example, if Post 1 was written in formal, standard English (e.g., \"I must ask you to leave my presence immediately as you are behaving irrationally\"), would you still rate it a 2/10, or would the absence of slang make it seem more \"serious\" or \"hostile\" to you? I'm trying to see if the model's \"toxicity\" detection is biased against certain ways of speaking.",
